    public string MaximumBinaryString(string binary) {
        int n = binary.Length;
        int ones = 0;
        int zeros = 0;
        for (char c in binary) {
            if (c == '1') ones++;
            else zeros++;
        }
        if (zeros == 0) return binary;
        if (zeros == 1) return binary;
        string res = new string('1', n);
        int pos = n - zeros;
        for (int i = 0; i < zeros - 1; i++) {
            res[pos + i + 1] = '0';
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```     The solution works by first counting the total number of 1's and 0's in the string. If there are no zeros or only one zero, the original string is already the maximum possible because no operations can improve it. For more than one zero, we know we can reduce the number of zeros to exactly 1 by repeatedly applying the operations, which effectively allows us to "eliminate" zeros by converting pairs of them into 1's while preserving the ability to position the remaining zero optimally.

The key insight is that the remaining zero can be placed at a position determined by the location of the first zero in the original string plus 1 (accounting for the fact that we can move zeros leftward through 1's).

